id cells in  the g0 or g1  phase for dividing cells you see a  smaller peak to the right that  represents  higher dna content as the cells are  duplicating their chromosomes in  preparation for mitosis this same  technique can be used to measure the  ploidy of the cell population as long as  you have a reference  diploid cell population for wrestling  the workflow for floyd analysis is  similar to what we did through  sequencing  first we dissociate ffte tissue using  the tissue dissociation kit with the  genital max octo with heaters  then we sting the cells with fermenting  cytokeratin  dappy and red blood cell marker cd235a  finally we analyze the samples by flow  cytometry on a max quant analyzer  we demonstrated polity analysis with two  fspe  samples a prostate carcinoma and a colon  carcinoma  the first dating step we performed was  to exclude red blood cells from the  analysis based on  positive cd-235-a staining  we also gated out non-nucleated cells by  the absence of dappy staining  as i mentioned before employee analysis  requires a reference diploid population  in this example we're actually able to  create our own reference population by  gating for  momentum positive cells in cytokera to  negative cells  uh conversely we're able to analyze the  tumor cells only by getting first  cytokeratin positive cells in fermented  negative cells  in the resulting dna histogram the  wrestling cells are shown as a green  trace whereas the carcinoma cells are  shown as the yellow trace  for the prostate sample the traces of  the carcinoma cells and the reference  cells are almost identical  this can be quantified as the dna index  which is simply the ratio of the dna  content of cancer cells to reference  cells  for the prostate sample the dna index is  close to one which indicates the  prostate carcinoma cells are not  unemployed  for the choline sample the traces for  the carcinoma cells and reference cells  are very different  for the colon carcinoma cells you see  two two peaks in spital one  the first peak peak one aligns with the  reference peak  this indicates that a portion of the  carcinoma cells are diploid  however the presence of the second peak  to the right of peak one indicates that  a portion of these cells have a higher  dna content than the reference cells and  so must be aneuploid  so as you can see this experiment  demonstrates the utility of the ffpe  tissue dissociation kit to generate a  max  control sample from the same ffpe block  when you don't have a max  normal sample available so that wraps up  all the experiments i wanted to show you  today  so i would just like to quickly review  what we  went over in today's webinar so the most  important thing to take home  is you can make a single cell suspension  out of fspe tissue  it's not well known but it's actually  very simple to do and we make it very  simple with our ffpe  dissociation kit um importantly when you  are dissociating the ffpe tissue if you  do want to do  downstream cell separation it will be  important to make sure that you are  preserving  your cell markers of interest and we've  demonstrated that we can preserve  the cytoskeletal markers of the mentin  and cytokeratin to separate out tumor  cells  uh or carcinoma cells from non-tumor  cells  we have also validated this workflow on  the analysis of dna  um so all of this allows you to reduce  tumor heterogeneity to get that more  sensitive  cancer-associated mutation detection  and it also importantly reveals uh  remove the need to always have  a matched normal tissue control with  your tumor samples which is not  available  in a lot of fspe archives  so that's all for today i'd like to  thank  everyone for listening and i'll now  open up for questions  awesome thank you so much karina for  your informative presentation  start we will now start the live q a  portion of the webinar if you have a  question you'd like to ask please do so  now just click on the ask a question box  located 